WEBVTT

1
-1:59:59,990 --> 00:00:04.634
How does a Vision Transformer pull classes apart, layer by layer?

2
00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:06.157
One number tracks it:

3
00:00:06.853 --> 00:00:12.287
separation fuzziness, the spread within each class relative to the distance between classes.

4
00:00:12.983 --> 00:00:14.597
Lower is better.

5
00:00:14.603 --> 00:00:18.225
On Fashion-MNIST, the raw images score about twenty-three.

6
00:00:18.829 --> 00:00:22.823
After the first block, fuzziness jumps to about ninety-seven.

7
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.568
Then it decays exponentially, block by block, to about two point seven.

8
00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:30.620
The paper calls this the Spike-Decay law.

9
00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:36.975
Over training, the last layer's fuzziness decays exponentially too, and settles near two point seven.

10
00:00:36.513 --> 00:00:41.784
Both laws hold across depths, attention heads, optimizers and four datasets.

11
00:00:42.481 --> 00:00:46.544
They break only when the embedding is too large for the model to learn.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:49.458
So most of the separation happens early.

13
00:00:50.061 --> 00:00:54.299
Depth should be just enough, and fuzziness could serve as a training monitor.
